    "steps": [
      {
        "id": "s1_characters",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Racemose — characters",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Characters",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "Racemose inflorescence — characters:",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          {
            "text": "The peduncle grows indefinitely; it never",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          "ends in a flower.",
          "On a long peduncle the flowers are acropetal:",
          "oldest at the base, youngest at the tip.",
          "On a condensed peduncle they are centripetal:",
          "oldest outside, youngest at the centre.",
          "Unbranched peduncle: simple inflorescence.",
          "Branched peduncle: compound inflorescence."
        ],
        "why": "Every racemose type below is this one rule in a different shape: the main axis keeps growing, so the youngest flowers are always at the tip (long axis) or at the centre (condensed axis).",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing basipetal for racemose. Basipetal order belongs to the cymose type.",
          "Saying the peduncle ends in a flower. That is the cymose character."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Racemose - the axis keeps Rising; acropetal - the Apex is youngest.",
        "margin_note": "The boxed indefinite-growth line plus acropetal order earns this mark."
      },
      {
        "id": "s2_raceme",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "1. Raceme",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Raceme",
        "lines": [
          "1. Raceme: the peduncle is long and bears",
          "bracteate, pedicellate flowers in acropetal",
          "order.",
          "Simple raceme: Crotalaria.",
          "Compound raceme (panicle): Mangifera (mango)."
        ],
        "why": "Raceme is the reference type - stalked (pedicellate) flowers on a long axis. Every other type is described by what changes from the raceme.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Forgetting that the compound raceme has its own name, panicle.",
          "Writing sessile. Raceme flowers have pedicels; sessile flowers make a spike."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Raceme is the base plan; a branched raceme is a panicle (mango).",
        "margin_note": "Definition plus both examples - simple and compound - for the full mark."
      },
      {
        "id": "s3_corymb",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "2. Corymb",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Corymb",
        "lines": [
          "2. Corymb: the older (lower) flowers have",
          "longer pedicels and the younger ones shorter,",
          "so all flowers are brought to the same level.",
          "Simple corymb: Cassia.",
          "Compound corymb: cauliflower."
        ],
        "why": "The corymb is a raceme with unequal pedicels doing a levelling job: lower flowers reach up on longer stalks, so the top of the cluster is flat.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Confusing corymb with umbel. In a corymb the pedicels start at DIFFERENT points on the axis; in an umbel they start at ONE point."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Corymb - a flat top made by longer stalks from lower down.",
        "margin_note": "The same-level line is the scoring phrase; cauliflower is the expected compound example."
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_umbel",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "3. Umbel",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Umbel",
        "lines": [
          "3. Umbel: the peduncle is condensed to a",
          "point; equal pedicellate flowers arise from",
          "that one point, covered at the base by an",
          "involucre of bracts.",
          "Simple umbel: onion.",
          "Compound umbel: carrot."
        ],
        "why": "Condensing the raceme axis to a single point turns it into an umbel - so all pedicels share one origin, like umbrella ribs, and the bracts crowd there as an involucre.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Leaving out the involucre of bracts - it is the marked feature of the umbel.",
          "Writing pedicels of different lengths. Umbel pedicels are about equal."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Umbel = umbrella: all ribs from one point. Onion simple, carrot compound.",
        "margin_note": "One point of origin + involucre + the two examples."
      },
      {
        "id": "s5_spike",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "4. Spike",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Spike",
        "lines": [
          "4. Spike: the long peduncle bears bracteate,",
          "sessile flowers in acropetal order.",
          "It is characteristic of the family Poaceae.",
          "Simple spike: Achyranthes.",
          "Compound spike: Oryza sativa (paddy)."
        ],
        "why": "A spike is a raceme whose flowers lost their stalks - sessile is the single scoring word. The compound spike of paddy ties it to Poaceae.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing pedicellate. Sessile (no pedicel) is what makes a spike a spike."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Spike - flowers sit straight on the axis, no stalk (sessile).",
        "margin_note": "The book prints 'Achyranthus'; write Achyranthes."
      },
      {
        "id": "s6_spadix_head",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "5. Spadix and 6. Head",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Spadix + head",
        "lines": [
          "5. Spadix: a fleshy peduncle bears sessile",
          "unisexual and neuter flowers, protected by a",
          "large modified bract called the spathe.",
          "Ex: Colocasia.",
          "6. Head: unisexual and bisexual sessile",
          "flowers develop centripetally on a condensed,",
          "flattened peduncle (receptacle).",
          "Ex: sunflower."
        ],
        "why": "Spadix = a spike wrapped in a spathe with unisexual flowers; head = the condensed-axis case, where the flat receptacle carries the youngest florets at its centre (centripetal).",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Forgetting the spathe - it is the identifying cover of the spadix.",
          "Writing acropetal for the head. On the condensed receptacle the order is centripetal."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Spadix wears a spathe; the head is a flat plate filled from the rim to the centre.",
        "margin_note": "Two types in one block: one defining line and one example each."
      },
